# Networking Fundamentals — Cheat Sheet

## Ports & Protocols
- IP address → reaches the server; **Port** → identifies the specific service; **Protocol** → defines the communication rules
- Runs mostly over the TCP/IP model

| Protocol |     Purpose                         |   Port |
|---|---|---|
| FTP      |   File transfer                     | 20 / 21 |
| SSH      |   Encrypted remote access           | 22 |
| SMTP     |   Sending email                     | 25 |
| DNS      |   Domain name → IP resolution       | 53 |
| HTTP     |   Web browsing (unencrypted)        | 80 |
| HTTPS    |   Web browsing (encrypted, SSL/TLS) | 443 |
| RDP      |   Windows remote administration     | 3389 |

- Firewalls: only open the ports actually needed, and only when needed (not permanently)
- Kerberos = common authentication protocol (identity verification)
- IDS/IPS: learn the normal traffic pattern per port/protocol, flag anomalies (e.g. sudden spikes)
- Future trends: IPv6 expansion, Zero Trust (every request always verified), quantum-safe encryption (physics-based, not math-based)

## TCP vs. UDP
Both are Layer 4 (Transport Layer) protocols — only one is used per given communication.

|               | TCP                                                           | UDP |
|---|---|---|
| Connection    | Connection-oriented (3-way handshake: SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK)     | Connectionless |
| Reliability   | High — checksums + acknowledgments, retransmits lost packets   | Low — no retransmission |
| Speed         | Slower (error checking overhead)                               | Faster |
| Typical use   | Web (HTTP/S), email (SMTP/IMAP/POP3), file transfer (FTP/SFTP) | Streaming, VoIP, video calls, gaming, DNS, IoT |
| Security      | TLS (HTTPS), firewall rules for trusted traffic                | Rate limiting, packet filtering, DNSSEC (for DNS) |

- Which protocol an app uses is hard-coded into the application itself — not something the end user chooses

## Switches & Routers
- **Switch**: connects devices *within* one LAN, uses MAC addresses, operates at Layer 2
  - Unmanaged = plug-and-play; Managed = supports VLANs + advanced security config
- **Router**: connects *different* networks/LANs together, uses IP addresses, operates at Layer 3
  - Static routing = manually configured paths; Dynamic routing = protocols like OSPF, BGP, EIGRP auto-select the best path
- A **Layer 3 switch** does both switching and routing (e.g. a typical home router)
- Best practices: change default credentials, role-based access control, VLANs for segmentation, QoS for bandwidth priority, SNMP/syslog monitoring, keep firmware updated, back up configs

## Access Points, Repeaters, Extenders
- **Access Point (AP)**: extends an existing *wired* network into wireless; needs an Ethernet connection; no bandwidth loss; used in offices/enterprise/public hotspots
- **Repeater**: rebroadcasts an existing Wi-Fi signal wirelessly only; reduces speed; plug-and-play; small homes/offices with minor dead zones
- **Extender**: extends coverage via wired backhaul (no speed loss) or wireless backhaul (same limitation as a repeater); larger buildings/areas with interference
- Always secure the wireless signal with a password; change default device credentials; keep firmware updated

## Patch Panels
- Centralized termination point for all cable runs (e.g. one per office) → then short cables connect the panel to the switches
- Types: copper (RJ45 connectors) or fiber optic; shielded (interference-prone areas) or unshielded (standard)
- Benefits: simplified troubleshooting/maintenance from one central point, easy scalability
- Best practice: label both ends of every connection, avoid kinks, leave slack, consider color-coding by section

## Firewalls
- A barrier between a trusted network and an untrusted one (e.g. the internet); filters traffic based on rules
- Inspection levels (basic → advanced):
  1. **Packet filtering** — IP/port only, simple and fast, limited security
  2. **Stateful inspection** — only allows traffic from an established/authenticated session
  3. **Next-gen (NGFW)** — deep packet inspection, built-in IDS/IPS, malware signature filtering
- Deployment types: **Network-based** (hardware, protects the whole network), **Host-based** (software, protects one system), **Cloud-based/FWaaS** (subscription, protects cloud systems)
- Best practices: least-privilege access to the firewall/config, review rules regularly, enable IDS/IPS where available

## Power over Ethernet (PoE)
- Delivers data *and* power over a single Ethernet cable — no separate power adapter needed
- Standards: **802.3af** (PoE, ≤15.4 W) → **802.3at** (PoE+, ≤30 W) → **802.3bt** (PoE++, ≤60–100 W)
- Needs a PoE-capable switch (or a PoE injector to retrofit a legacy setup)
- Limits: thin copper wiring caps deliverable power; standard Ethernet runs capped at 100 m
- Use cases: IP cameras, wireless access points, VoIP phones, smart buildings, industrial IoT sensors

## Cable & DSL Modems
Both are technically Layer 3 switches — they route your home network to the internet while also providing local switching ports/Wi-Fi.

|             | Cable                                                   | DSL |
|---|---|---|
| Medium      | Coaxial cable                                           | Telephone line |
| Standard    | DOCSIS (up to 10 Gbps)                                  | ADSL (asymmetric) / VDSL (up to ~100 Mbps) |
| Bandwidth   | Shared — congestion possible at peak times              | Dedicated per subscriber |
| Best for    | High-bandwidth use (streaming, gaming, large downloads) | Basic browsing/email; rural areas without cable/fiber |

- Best practices: keep away from heat/electronic interference, change default credentials, enable firewall/encryption, restart to resolve most connectivity issues

## Optical Network Terminals (ONTs)
- Converts the fiber optic signal into Ethernet — the key device for Fiber to the Premises/Home (FTTP/FTTH)
- At home: the ONT is essentially built into your router; in business: a separate device feeding the existing routers/switches
- Faster (Gigabit+) and more reliable than cable/DSL — uses light, so it's immune to electromagnetic interference
- May include voice ports (RJ11) and PoE support, depending on the model
- Best practices: secure default credentials, keep firmware updated; the provider typically handles activation/IP assignment

## Network Interface Cards (NICs)
- Hardware that lets a device connect to a network, wired or wireless — integrated on most laptops/desktops, often a dedicated card on servers (better performance)
- Config options: static or DHCP-assigned IP, NIC teaming/load balancing (more bandwidth + redundancy), Wake-on-LAN, MTU tuning, jumbo frames, QoS
- Wired: faster (up to 100 Gbps), more stable, less interference/range issues — desktops, servers, gaming
- Wireless: more convenient, up to ~10 Gbps (Wi-Fi 6E), susceptible to interference — laptops, mobile, IoT
- Security: MAC address filtering, disable any unused interface

## Network Topologies
- **Bus**: devices connected in a line, terminator needed at the end (early coax networks)
- **Ring**: devices connected in a loop; data circulates until it reaches its destination
- **Star**: centralized device (e.g. a switch), everything connects to it — most common today, scales well
- **Mesh**: every device connected to every other device — very high reliability, doesn't scale (used for small groups of critical servers)
- **Hybrid**: a combination of two or more topologies — typical in enterprise networks
- **Wireless**: no cabling at all
- Future trends: Software-Defined Networking (reconfigure via software, common in cloud data centers), 5G-enabled topologies, AI-driven network management

## Subnetting
- Splits a large network into smaller, more manageable sub-networks → better IP allocation, improved security/isolation, less congestion
- IPv4 address = 32 bits; the **subnet mask** marks the network portion vs. the host portion (e.g. `/24` = 24 network bits, 8 host bits)
- **CIDR notation** (e.g. `/26`) = number of bits allocated to the network portion
- Formulas:
  - Number of subnets = 2ⁿ (n = borrowed bits)
  - Usable hosts per subnet = 2ʰ − 2 (h = remaining host bits)
- In every subnet, the **first address** = network address and the **last address** = broadcast address — neither is assignable to a host
- Private IP ranges (e.g. `192.168.x.x`) never touch the public internet directly — only the router's external interface has a public IP
- IPv6: 128-bit addresses (vastly larger space), uses a "network prefix" instead of a subnet mask, supports **SLAAC** for self-configuration (no DHCP required)
